                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC4READ</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECCAREAD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>WBFILL</name>
                <description>Filling state for the write-buffer data words. E.g. WBFILL = 0000 (Write-Buffer invalid), WBFILL = 0011 (Write-Buffer intermediate), WBFILL = 1111 (Write-Buffer valid)

The bits of WBFILL are set by the hardware, when write, verify-only, or forming data is written into the write-buffer.
WBFILL is reset after each direct write, online-repair, or hardening operation.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC1MRD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC2MRD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC3MRD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC4MRD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECCAMRD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CONFSTATE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[26:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OLRFLAG</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HARDFLAG</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>REREA</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>WSUSP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_PROG</name>
            <description>programming control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xF137FF0F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ACTION</name>
                <description>Selects indirect write, verify-only, hardening or forming operations:
0000: Idle state, no action triggered. Writing '0000' exits current mode.
0001: Select indirect write operation, using algorithm selected by SELAL, NVM_SELALGO1/2.SEL*ALGO1/2/3, and NVM_SETUP1.FSMSEQ.
0010: Select verify-only: Written data is compared to array content.
0100: Select hardening: Existing data is verified and hardened/corrected if 
necessary.
1000: Select forming operation, using algorithm selected by NVM_SELALGO2.SELFALGO.
1110: Select looped write operation, using algorithm selected by SLSTRAL, NVM_SELALGO1/2.SEL*ALGO1/2/3, and NVM_SETUP1.FSMSEQ; 
cannot be set, if NVM_ACC.ACC2_EN = '0'.
Note: Only to be used in production test flow.
0111: Select looped forming operation, using algorithm selected by NVM_SELALGO2.SELFALGO; 
cannot be set, if NVM_ACC.ACC2_EN = '0'.
Note: Only to be used in production test flow.
1011: Select operation 'retrieve configuration settings' from configuration space in extra area;
cannot be set, if NVM_ACC.ACC3_EN = '0'.
Others: Interpreted and stored in SFR as '0000'
The operation selected by ACTION is performed, when STARTOP = '11' is written while ACTION stays constant. NVM_ADDR 
defines the address for the operation.
Verification results can be read at NVM_STATUS.VERR.
ACTION can only be changed to a value other than '0000', if the current value of ACTION is '0000'. Otherwise ACTION = '0000' is stored. Once 
ACTION is set to a value other than '0000', ACTION can only be written again with its current value; any other value leads to a reset of ACTION to '0000'</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SELRCS</name>
                <description>Select parameter for operation 'retrieve configuration settings' from configuration space in extra area.
0: Retrieve SYSCONF information and configuration settings, but do not retrieve write algorithm information.
1: Retrieve SYSCONF information, configuration settings, and write algorithm information.
Note: SELRCS only applies to the sequence explicitly started with ACTION = '1011', but not to the implicitly started sequences during wake-up and write sequences</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTTRAP</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
0: No reset of NVM ready trap on nvm_trap_req_o.
1: Reset of NVM ready trap on nvm_trap_req_o.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTIR</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
0: No reset of ECC logging interrupt on nvm_ir_ecclog_req_o.
1: Reset of ECC logging interrupt on nvm_ir_ecclog_req_o.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RBINVAL</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
0: No invalidation of read-buffer content.
1: Invalidation of read-buffer content.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTVERR</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
0: No reset of NVM_STATUS.VERR, NVM_STATUS.HARDFLAG, and NVM_STATUS.OLRFLAG.
1: Reset of NVM_STATUS.VERR, NVM_STATUS.HARDFLAG, and NVM_STATUS.OLRFLAG.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTECC</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
0: No reset of NVM_STATUS.ECC*READ, NVM_STATUS.REREA, NVM_STATUS.RWWERR, and NVM_STATUS.WRPERR.
1: Reset of NVM_STATUS.ECC*READ, NVM_STATUS.REREA, NVM_STATUS.RWWERR, and NVM_STATUS.WRPERR.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STARTOP</name>
                <description>Can only be set by software, is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle.
00: No start of operation defined by ACTION.
11: Start operation defined by ACTION, if these are not changed with the same write operation that sets STARTOP.
Others: Interpreted and stored in SFR as '00'.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OLREN</name>
                <description>000: disable online-repair (OLR).
001: enable online-repair for &gt;= single-bit write or forming fail in block.
010: enable online-repair for &gt;= double-bit write or forming fail in block.
011: enable online-repair for&gt;= triple-bit write or forming fail in block.
1xx: enable online-repair for &gt; triple-bit write or forming fail in block.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SELAL</name>
                <description>Select type of write algorithm:
00: Employ first (typically standard, 8 bit parallel) algorithm defined by NVM_SELALGO1.SEL*ALGO1.
01: Employ second (typically strong, 8 bit parallel) algorithm defined by NVM_SELALGO1.SEL*ALGO2.
1x: Employ third (typically standard, 1 bit parallel) algorithm defined by NVM_SELALGO2.SEL*ALGO3.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RDWB</name>
                <description>Read from write-buffer:
0: disabled.
1: enabled for memory bus reads with ACTION = ['0001', '0010', '1000', '1110', '0111'] and read address matching content of NVMADDR.
RDWB is automatically reset, when the content of the write-buffer is invalidated or when ACTION is changed.</description>
                <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEMP</name>
                <description>actual junction temperature:
0000: invalid/unknown junction temperature value.
others: junction temperature value to be used for voltage compensation in programming algorithms.
Example for coding:
0001: Tj &lt;= 50C
0010: 50C &lt; Tj &lt;= 85C 
0100: 85C &lt; Tj &lt;= 100C
1000: 100C &lt; Tj &lt;= 110C
1010: 110C &lt; Tj &lt;=120C
1100: Tj &gt; 120C
Note: The coding has to use increasing values of TEMP for increasing temperatures and it has to match to the coding employed in NVM_COMPEN.TMIN*.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_ADDR</name>
            <description>address register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRB</name>
                <description>block address for operations started with NVM_PROG.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRWL</name>
                <description>wordline address for operations started with NVM_PROG.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRS</name>
                <description>sector address for operations started with NVM_PROG.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_CONF1</name>
            <description>configuration register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x8020</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x83FFC073</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>HRLEV</name>
                <description>defines single hardread level for verify-only operation started for NVM_PROG.ACTION = '0010':
00: normal read.
01: hardread reset.
1x: hardread set.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DWVERR</name>
                <description>for direct write operations, defines the threshold for a verify error indication:
000: verify is pass only, if no fail bit.
001: verify is pass only, if at most a single-bit fail.
010: verify is pass only, if at most a double-bit fail.
011: verify is pass only, if at most a triple-bit fail.
1xx: verify is always pass, independent of any fail bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TRAP_ON</name>
                <description>0: no NVM ready traps are generated on nvm_trap_req_o.
1: NVM ready traps are generated when long lasting NVM actions (indirect write, hardening, forming, and wake-up) are finished
Note: Direct write operations and verify-only operations never generate a ready trap.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NVM_ON</name>
                <description>0: NVM is switched to or stays in NVM sleep mode.
1: NVM is switched to or stays in normal mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SECPROT</name>
                <description>number of write protected sectors, starting with sector 0.
SECPROT cannot be changed, if WPLCK = '1'.</description>
                <bitRange>[25:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>WPLCK</name>
                <description>Locking of protection settings in SECPROT:
0: SECPROT can be changed.
1: SECPROT cannot be changed.
WPLCK cannot be written by software, if it is '1'.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_HARDEN1</name>
            <description>hardening configuration register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x81FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>BLOCKS</name>
                <description>BLOCKS+1 defines number of blocks in address range selected for hardening:
start of hardening address range = NVM_ADDR
end of hardening address range = NVM_ADDR + BLOCKS
only valid for ALLMEM = '0'</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ALLMEM</name>
                <description>selected address range for hardening operation: 
0: defined by NVM_ADDR and BLOCKS.
1: complete memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_HARDADDR</name>
            <description>hardening address register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3FFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRB</name>
                <description>block address for automatic hardening</description>
                <bitRange>[4:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRWL</name>
                <description>wordline address for automatic hardening</description>
                <bitRange>[7:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ADDRS</name>
                <description>sector address for automatic hardening</description>
                <bitRange>[17:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_HRESP</name>
            <description>bus response register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x8007</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>HRESP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[2:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTHRESP</name>
                <description>reset of NVM_HRESP: 
0: no reset of HRESP triggered trigger 
1: reset of HRESP 
RSTHRESP is reset automatically by hardware after one clock cycle</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_ECCLOG</name>
            <description>ECC failure logging register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ECC1CNT</name>
                <description>Number of ECC single-bit failures detected and corrected during the last memory read operations (or the initial reading of existing data for a write operation).
ECC1CNT is only updated, if NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '0x' and if even the last re-read operation (configured in NVM_SETUP1.REREAD) yields an ECC single-bit failure.
For NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '1x', ECC1CNT stays unchanged.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC2CNT</name>
                <description>Number of ECC double-bit failures detected and corrected during the last memory read operations (or the initial reading of existing data for a write operation).
ECC2CNT is only updated, if NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '0x' and if even the last re-read operation (configured in NVM_SETUP1.REREAD) yields an ECC double-bit failure.
For NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '1x', ECC2CNT stays unchanged.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC3CNT</name>
                <description>Number of ECC triple-bit failures detected and corrected during the last memory read operations (or the initial reading of existing data for a write operation).
ECC3CNT is only updated, if NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '0x' and if even the last re-read operation (configured in NVM_SETUP1.REREAD) yields an ECC triple-bit failure.
For NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '1x', ECC3CNT stays unchanged.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC4CNT</name>
                <description>Number of ECC-uncorrectable multi-bit failures detected during the last memory read operations (or the initial reading of existing data for a write operation).
ECC4CNT is only updated, if NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '0x' and if even the last re-read operation (configured in NVM_SETUP1.REREAD) still yields an ECC-uncorrectable multi-bit failure.
For NVM_TEST1.ECCOFFRD = '1x', ECC4CNT stays unchanged.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_ECCLOGIR</name>
            <description>ECC failure logging interrupt register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0xFFFFFFFF</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ECC1CNTIR</name>
                <description>Interrupt threshold for number of ECC single-bit failures indicated in NVM_ECCLOG.ECC1CNT:
If NVM_ECCLOG.ECC1CNT becomes larger than ECC1CNTIR, an ECC logging interrupt is triggered.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC2CNTIR</name>
                <description>Interrupt threshold for number of ECC double-bit failures indicated in NVM_ECCLOG.ECC2CNT:
If NVM_ECCLOG.ECC2CNT becomes larger than ECC2CNTIR, an ECC logging interrupt is triggered.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC3CNTIR</name>
                <description>Interrupt threshold for number of ECC triple-bit failures indicated in NVM_ECCLOG.ECC3CNT:
If NVM_ECCLOG.ECC3CNT becomes larger than ECC3CNTIR, an ECC logging interrupt is triggered</description>
                <bitRange>[27:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ECC4CNTIR</name>
                <description>Interrupt threshold for number of ECC-uncorrectable multi-bit failures indicated in NVM_ECCLOG.ECC4CNT:
If NVM_ECCLOG.ECC4CNT becomes larger than ECC4CNTIR, an ECC logging interrupt is triggered.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>NVM_VMODE</name>
            <description>voltage mode register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VMODE</name>
                <description>voltage/frequency mode for operations:
00: undefined / unknown
01: ULP mode
10: MF mode
11: LP mode</description>
                <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>RAMC_PPU0</name>
      <description>Power Policy Unit Registers for System RAM</description>
      <headerStructName>RAMC_PPU</headerStructName>
      <baseAddress>0x42220000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>4096</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>PWPR</name>
          <description>Power Policy Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x108</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x10F110F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_POLICY</name>
              <description>Power mode policy.  When static power mode transitions are enabled, PWR_DYN_EN is set to 0, this is the target power mode for the PPU.  When dynamic power mode transitions are enabled, PWR_DYN_EN is set to 1, this is the minimum power mode for the PPU.

This PPU supports the following modes: OFF(0), MEM_RET(2), ON(8).  Do not use WARM_RST(9) or other unsupported modes.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_DYN_EN</name>
              <description>Power mode dynamic transition enable.  When this bit is set to 1 dynamic transitions are enabled for power modes, allowing transitions to be initiated by changes on power mode DEVACTIVE inputs.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOCK_EN</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OP_POLICY</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OP_DYN_EN</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PMER</name>
          <description>Power Mode Emulation Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>EMU_EN</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PWSR</name>
          <description>Power Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x10F110F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_STATUS</name>
              <description>Power mode status.  These bits reflect the current power mode of the PPU.  See PPU_PWPR.PWR_POLICY for power mode enumeration.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_DYN_STATUS</name>
              <description>Power mode dynamic transition status.  When set to 1 power mode dynamic transitions are enabled.  There might be a delay in dynamic transitions becoming active or inactive if the PPU is transitioning when PWR_DYN_EN is programmed.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>LOCK_STATUS</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OP_STATUS</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OP_DYN_STATUS</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DISR</name>
          <description>Device Interface Input Current Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFF0007FF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PWR_DEVACTIVE_STATUS</name>
              <description>Status of the power mode DEVACTIVE inputs.

There is one bit for each device interface Q-Channel DEVQACTIVE.  For example, bit 0 is for Q-channel device 0 DEVQACTIVE.  Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>OP_DEVACTIVE_STATUS</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>MISR</name>
          <description>Miscellaneous Input Current Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFF01</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PCSMPACCEPT_STATUS</name>
              <description>The status of the PCSMPACCEPT input.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DEVACCEPT_STATUS</name>
              <description>Status of the device interface DEVACCEPT inputs.  

There is one bit for each device interface DEVQACCEPTn.  For example, bit 8 is for Q-Channel 0 DEVQACCEPTn and bit 9 for Q-Channel 1 DEVQACCEPTn.   Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DEVDENY_STATUS</name>
              <description>Status of the device interface DEVDENY inputs.  

There is one bit for each device interface DEVQDENY.  For example, bit 16 is for Q-Channel 0 DEVQDENY, and bit 17 for Q-Channel 1 DEVQDENY.  Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>STSR</name>
          <description>Stored Status Register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
