              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>ACTRLR</name>
      <description>Autonomnous Controller</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42ED0000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>CTRL</name>
          <description>control register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>ENABLED</name>
              <description>- 0: LPPASS IP disabled
- 1: LPPASS IP enabled

This field functions as the Global Enable for the LPPASS IP.  This field must be set to gain read/write access to any other register in the LPPASS memory map.

During initial IP release from reset (i.e. during POR), the LPPASS IP (along with its switched internal power domain) is disabled.

After setting this bit, the LPPASS internal switched domain is powered up.  The user must then wait for PWR_STATUS.SW_OK to assert.  At this point, the user has read/write access to all LPPASS memory map registers.

Clearing this field (i.e. disabling the LPPASS) can lead to status only clearing, or status and configuration clearing (see CFG.DISABLE_MODE).</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CMD_RUN</name>
          <description>run command register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>RUN</name>
              <description>Autonomous Controller Run Command

Firmware should only write to this field when STATUS=STOPPED or PAUSED.

When firmware sets this field, the Autonomous Controller starts running at the State pointed to by CMD_STATE.  Hardware clears this field when the Autonomous Controller acknowledges the run command.  If this field is set while STATUS=RUNNING, the Autonomous Controller will not respond to the command (beyond clearing the field).

This register is reset whenever the following occurs:

1.  The Autonomous Controller is disabled (clearing CTRL.ENABLED)
2.  The LPPASS is configured for HS mode (STATUS.MODE=0) and the chip leaves an Active Mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CMD_STATE</name>
          <description>state override register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x3F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>STATE</name>
              <description>Autonomous Controller Command State 

This field indicates where which AC State (0-63) the AC will execute instructions from when it receives a RUN or SINGLE_STEP command.  Hardware updates this field whenever the AC transitions from RUNNING to STOPPED or PAUSED.  When STOPPED or PAUSED, firmware can overrwrite this field to force the AC to execute from a specific STATE when triggered by a subsequent RUN or SINGLE_STEP command.  

This field must never be written to if STATUS=RUNNING.

This register is reset whenever the following occurs:

1.  The Autonomous Controller is disabled (clearing CTRL.ENABLED)
2.  The LPPASS is configured for HS mode (STATUS.MODE=0) and the chip leaves an Active Mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>FW_TR[%s]</name>
          <description>firmware trigger</description>
          <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>FW_TR</name>
              <description>Firmware Trigger 

This field is used as a firmware trigger for the Autonomous Controller.  The Autonomous Controller can be programmed to respond to the firmware trigger by programming a state with a condtion (COND) TR_LPPASS_IN*.  Additionally, writing to this field will force the Autonomus Controller to wake from sleep. Hardware clears this bit when the Autonomous Controller acknowledges the trigger.

This register is reset whenever the following occurs:

1.  The Autonomous Controller is disabled (clearing CTRL.ENABLED)
2.  The LPPASS is configured for HS mode (STATUS.MODE=0) and the chip leaves an Active Mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>STATUS</name>
          <description>status register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x73F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CUR_STATE</name>
              <description>This register indicates the current state of the Autonomous Controller (valid values are 0-63).</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>STATUS</name>
              <description>Autonomous Controller status</description>
              <bitRange>[9:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>STOPPED</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller is stopped</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RUNNING</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller is running</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>PAUSED</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller is paused</description>
                  <value>2</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>RSVD</name>
                  <description>N/A</description>
                  <value>3</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>MODE</name>
              <description>This field indicates the mode of the Autonomous Controller.  The Autonmous Controller always resets to HS mode.

This field is reset whenever the following occurs:

1.  The Autonomous Controller is disabled (clearing CTRL.ENABLED)
2.  The LPPASS is configured for HS mode (STATUS.MODE=0) and the chip leaves an Active Mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
              <enumeratedValues>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>HS</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller is in High Speed (HS) mode

In this mode, the LPPASS IP is functional only in chip Active modes.  The LPPASS IP is clocked from a peripheral source derived from clk_hf.</description>
                  <value>0</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
                <enumeratedValue>
                  <name>LP</name>
                  <description>Autonomous Controller is Low Power (LP) mode

In this mode, the LPPASS IP is functional in both chip Active and Deepsleep modes.  The LPPASS IP is clocked from the internal LPOSC.</description>
                  <value>1</value>
                </enumeratedValue>
              </enumeratedValues>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>CFG</name>
          <description>config register (only accessible if CTRL.EN=0)</description>
          <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x3000F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>DOUT_EN</name>
              <description>DOUT output enable.  

Bits in this field must be set prior to writing to TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CF0_DOUT.
   
-0 : DOUT[X] output disabled  
- 1: DOUT[X] output enabled</description>
              <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SLEEP_MODE</name>
              <description>Autonomous Controller Sleep Mode

-0 : Keep the LPPASS switched digital supply powered when the AC is sleeping  (i.e. AC state programmed to action (ACT)=WAIT_FOR,  and condition (COND)=wakeup source).  

- 1: Power Down the LPPASS digital supply when AC is asleep (i.e. AC ACT=WAIT_FOR, AC_COND= wakeup source).  This has the following consequences to the LPPASS:
     1.  Digital leakage is reduced (i.e. all logic on the LPPASS switched supply is powered off).
     2.  CTBLs, SAR FIRs, and the DACs loose all configuration (i.e. they must be re-programmed to be functional again).
     3.  The wakeup time is increased by 3us ( to account for the switched power domain coming up).</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DISABLE_MODE</name>
              <description>Autonomous Controller Disable Mode

-0 : Keep the LPPASS switched digital supply powered when the AC is disabled ( i.e. CTRL.ENABLED=0).  

- 1: Power Down the LPPASS digital supply when AC is disabled.  This has the following consequences to the LPPASS:
     1.  Digital leakage is reduced (i.e. all logic on the LPPASS switched supply is powered off).
     2.  All LPPASS configuration is lost (all registers are reset and SRAMs lose data).</description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DEBUG_PAUSE</name>
          <description>pause debug register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x30</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>PAUSE</name>
              <description>Pause Command

Setting this field requests the Autonomous Controller (AC) to pause.  

This field is used for debug purposes only.  

The AC waits for the present state to complete before pausing.  If the present state cannot complete (i.e. the ACT and COND are programmed improperly), the AC will not pause.  This command must not be used when executing an AC sleep state (ACT=WAIT_FOR and COND=WAKE_*).  

When the AC is paused, all AC registerd conditions are cleared.

Hardware clears this bit when the Autonomous Controller is paused.  This command is ignored if STATUS.STATUS is PAUSED or STOP.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>DEBUG_SINGLE_STEP</name>
          <description>single step debug register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x34</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-write</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SINGLE_STEP</name>
              <description>Single Step Command

Setting this field requests the Autonomous Controller (AC) to execute the next AC state (pointed to by CMD_STATE.STATE) and pause after completing that state.  

This field is used for debug purposes only.  

If the state (pointed to by CMD_STATE.STATE) cannot complete (i.e. the ACT and COND are programmed improperly), the AC will not pause at the end of the single step command.  This command must not be used when executing an AC sleep state (ACT=WAIT_FOR and COND=*_WAKE).  

When the AC is paused, all AC registerd conditions are cleared.

Hardware clears this bit when the Autonomous Controller pauses after executing the single step command.  This command is ignored if STATUS.STATUS is RUNNING.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <name>PWR_STATUS</name>
          <description>power status register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x38</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>SW_OK</name>
              <description>This field indicates the status of the LPPASS switched power domain.  This status should be polled whenever CTRL.EN toggles from a 0 to 1.

0: switched domain is powered off.
1: switched domain is powered on.</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
          <dim>4</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
          <name>CNTR_STATUS[%s]</name>
          <description>Loop/Interval counter status register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x100</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0x800FFF3F</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CUR_STATE</name>
              <description>This field indicates the assigned Autonomous Controller (AC) State of a counter.  Valid numbers are 0-63.</description>
              <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CUR_CNT</name>
              <description>This field indicates the count value for the given counter.</description>
              <bitRange>[19:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>BUSY</name>
              <description>This field indicates if the counter is busy (i.e. count value is non-zero).</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <cluster>
          <dim>64</dim>
          <dimIncrement>32</dimIncrement>
          <name>TTCFG[%s]</name>
          <description>Timer Table Structure  (only accessible if CTRL.EN=0)</description>
          <addressOffset>0x00000800</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>TT_CFG0</name>
            <description>AC Table Configuration 0</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x1FF1F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DOUT</name>
                <description>LPPASS data out generation (the corresponding bit in CFG.DOUT_EN must be set when using this field)

The data out bus is driven directly to the GPIO HSIO interface.  The HSIO interface must be programmed properly for the DOUT bus to be observed on the GPIO pins.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MODE</name>
                <description>LPPASS Operating Mode
0: High Speed (HS) Operation -  The PASS is clocked from CLK_HF directly (nominally 80MHz) and is only functional in chip Active mode (i.e. the IP is reset in chip Deepsleep and Hibernate)
1: Low Power (LP) Operation - The PASS is clocked from CLK_LPOSC (4MHz) and is functional in chip Active and Deepsleep mode (i.e. the IP is only reset in Hibernate)</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MODE_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>Mode Unlock
0: Lock: MODE does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: MODE does get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PRB_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>PRB Unlock
0: Lock: PRB fields: VREF1(0)_VAL, VREF1(0)_FW_MODE do NOT get updated
1: Unlock: PRB fields: VREF1(0)_VAL, VREF1(0)_FW_MODE do get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SAR_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>SAR Unlock
0: Lock: SAR fields: EN does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: SAR fields: EN does get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC0_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>DAC0 Unlock
0: Lock: DAC0 fields: EN does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: DAC0 fields: EN does get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DAC1_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>See DAC0_UNLOCK</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PTC_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>PTC Unlock
0: Lock: PTC fields: CMP1(0)_EN, CMP1(0)_CFG do NOT get updated
1: Unlock: PTC fields: CMP1(0)_EN, CMP1(0)_CFG do get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CTBL0_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>CTBL0 Unlock
0: Lock: CTBL0 fields: OPA1(0)_EN, OPA1(0)_GAIN, OPA1(0)_CFG do NOT get updated
1: Unlock: CTBL0 fields: OPA1(0)_EN, OPA1(0)_GAIN, OPA1(0)_CFG do get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CTBL1_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>See CTBL0_UNLOCK</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DOUT_UNLOCK</name>
                <description>DOUT Unlock
0: Lock: DOUT does NOT get updated
1: Unlock: DOUT does get updated</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>TT_CFG1</name>
            <description>AC Table Configuration 1</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>BR_ADDR</name>
                <description>absolute address of the branch if taken (0-63 possible).

This field in only used if the AC action (ACT) is set to BRANCH_IF_* and the condtion (COND) is met.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD_0</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[7:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>COND</name>
                <description>Autonomous Controller Condition</description>
                <bitRange>[13:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>False</name>
                    <description>Hardcoded FALSE value</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>True</name>
                    <description>Hardcoded TRUE value</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BLOCK_READY</name>
                    <description>Enabled block(s) are ready for use.</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CNT_DONE</name>
                    <description>if the ACTION is WAIT_FOR - this is an interval timer.  
If the ACTION is BRANCH IF - this is a loop counter.</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR Sequencer Done</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_EOS</name>
                    <description>SAR Sequencer EOS</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_RANGE0</name>
                    <description>SAR Range 0 detection</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_RANGE1</name>
                    <description>SAR Range 1 detection</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_RANGE2</name>
                    <description>SAR Range 2 detection</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_RANGE3</name>
                    <description>SAR Range 3 detection</description>
                    <value>9</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_BUSY</name>
                    <description>SAR BUSY detection

This status is set whenever the SAR is running a scan.  This status is transparent from the SAR sequencer (i.e. the status is not sticky).</description>
                    <value>10</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR0_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR FIR0 Done detection</description>
                    <value>11</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR1_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR FIR1 Done detection</description>
                    <value>12</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIFO_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR FIFO Done</description>
                    <value>13</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR0_FIFO_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR FIR0 FIFO Done detection</description>
                    <value>14</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SAR_FIR1_FIFO_DONE</name>
                    <description>SAR FIR1 FIFO Done detection</description>
                    <value>15</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
