                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>VBG</name>
                    <description>reference derived from VBG</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>VDDA</name>
                    <description>reference derived from VDDA</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>VAL</name>
                <description>TAP location for PRB VREF</description>
                <bitRange>[7:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_0</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_1</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_2</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_3</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_4</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>4</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_5</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>5</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_6</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>6</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_7</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>7</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_8</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>8</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_9</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>9</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_10</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>10</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_11</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>11</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_12</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>12</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_13</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>13</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_14</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>14</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TAP_15</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>15</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EN</name>
                <description>PRB VREF enable control</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>TIMER</name>
          <description>Wakeup Timer configuration (only accessible if ACTRLR.STATUS.MODE=0)</description>
          <addressOffset>0x00000400</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>CTRL</name>
            <description>Timer trigger control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ENABLED</name>
                <description>0=disabled, 1=enabled

This timer (downcounter) is used by the Autonomous Controller (AC) as a wakeup source ( Autonomous Controller Action (ACT) = WAIT_FOR and Condition (COND) = TIMER_DONE_WAKE).

Even if CTRL.ENABLED is set, the Timer is held in Reset until the Autonomous Controller is running (ACTRLR.STATUS.STATUS= RUNNING).  The Timer automatically resets whenenever the Autonomous Controller transitions between HS and LP modes, is paused, or disabled. The timer will generate a wakeup signal to the AC whenever it reaches Terminal Count (0).</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CFG</name>
            <description>Timer trigger configuration register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>CLOCK_SEL</name>
                <description>Select Clock source of the Timer</description>
                <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLK_LPOSC</name>
                    <description>Timer clocked from CLK_LPOSC (nominally 4.096MHz)</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLK_LF</name>
                    <description>Timer clocked from CLK_LF (nominally 32kHz)</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TBD_0</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TBD_1</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PERIOD</name>
            <description>Timer trigger period register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PER_VAL</name>
                <description>Actual timer period is PER_VAL+1 (1 to 65536).</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
    <peripheral>
      <name>LPPASS_MMIO</name>
      <description>LPPASS top-level MMIO</description>
      <baseAddress>0x42EF0000</baseAddress>
      <addressBlock>
        <offset>0</offset>
        <size>65536</size>
        <usage>registers</usage>
      </addressBlock>
      <registers>
        <register>
          <name>INTR_CAUSE</name>
          <description>Interrupt cause register</description>
          <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
          <size>32</size>
          <access>read-only</access>
          <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
          <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
          <fields>
            <field>
              <name>CTBL0_COMP0_INT</name>
              <description>CTBL0 Comparator 0 Interrupt Pending

 It is set by the CTBL0 Opamp0 Comparator if the condition is met (i.e. CTBL_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP0_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTBL0_COMP1_INT</name>
              <description>CTBL0 Comparator 1 Interrupt Pending

 It is set by the CTBL0 Opamp1 Comparator if the condition is met (i.e. CTBL_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP1_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTBL1_COMP0_INT</name>
              <description>CTBL1 Comparator 0 Interrupt Pending

 It is set by the CTBL1 Opamp0 Comparator if the condition is met (i.e. CTBL_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP0_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTBL1_COMP1_INT</name>
              <description>CTBL1 Comparator 1 Interrupt Pending

 It is set by the CTBL1 Opamp1 Comparator if the condition is met (i.e. CTBL_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP1_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_COMP0_INT</name>
              <description>PTC Comparator 0 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the PTC Comparator 0 if the condition is met (i.e. PTC_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP0_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_COMP1_INT</name>
              <description>PTC Comparator 1 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the PTC Comparator 1 if the condition is met (i.e. PTC_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP1_OUT field).</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_RANGE0_INT</name>
              <description>PTC Post Processor 0 Range Interrupt Pending

It is set by the PTC Post Processor if the RANGE0 condition is met (i.e. PTC_PP_STRUCT0.*).</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_RANGE1_INT</name>
              <description>PTC Post Processor 1 Range Interrupt Pending

It is set by the PTC Post Processor if the RANGE0 condition is met (i.e. PTC_PP_STRUCT1.*).</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE0_INT</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 0 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC0 if the RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_0).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE1_INT</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 1 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC0 if the RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_1).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE2_INT</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 2 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC0 if the RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_2).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_EPOCH_INT</name>
              <description>DAC0 End of Waveform Interrupt Pending

It is set by DAC0 when finishing a waveform.  This condition is only used if the DAC0 channel (pointed to by the AC when triggered) is configured for LUT mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_EMPTY_INT</name>
              <description>DAC0 Empty Interrupt Pending

It is set by DAC0 when it empties the contents of its FW_DAC_VAL register.  This interrupt is only generated if DAC0 is configured for FW operation (ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CFG3.DAC0_CHANNEL=FW_CHANNEL.</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE0_INT</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 0 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC1 if the RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_0).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE1_INT</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 1 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC1 if the RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_1).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE2_INT</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 2 Interrupt Pending

It is set by the DAC1 if the RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_2).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_EPOCH_INT</name>
              <description>DAC1 End of Waveform Interrupt Pending

It is set by DAC1 when finishing a waveform.  This condition is only used if the DAC0 channel (pointed to by the AC when triggered) is configured for LUT mode.</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_EMPTY_INT</name>
              <description>DAC1 Empty Interrupt Pending

It is set by DAC1 when it empties the contents of its FW_DAC_VAL register.  This interrupt is only generated if DAC1 is configured for FW operation (ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CFG3.DAC1_CHANNEL=FW_CHANNEL.</description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_DONE_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Sequencer Done Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR when finishing a state in the SAR sequencer table (HS or LP) where SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to STOP.  The interrupt is not generated if the SAR is configured for CONTINUOUS operation (i.e. SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to GO_TO_ENTRY_ADDRESS).  

This interrupt does NOT indicate that the SAR data has been written to the FIFO (the FIFO level interrupts should be used instead).</description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_EOS_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Sequencer EOS Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR when finishing the state in the SAR sequencer table (HS or LP) where SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to GO_TO_ENTRY_ADDRESS or STOP.  

This interrupt does NOT indicate that the SAR data has been written to the FIFO (the FIFO level interrupts should be used instead).</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RESULT_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Result Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR when a channel writes data into its associated result register.  Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.CHAN_RESULT_UPDATED to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.CHAN_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the SAR to generate the interrupt source.</description>
              <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE0_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Range 0 detection Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR if the RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_0).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.</description>
              <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE1_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Range 1 detection Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR if the RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_1).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE2_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Range 2 detection Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR if the RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_2).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE3_INT</name>
              <description>SAR Range 3 detection Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the SAR if the RANGE3 condition is met for a given channel with range 3 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_3).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_FIR0_RESULT_INT</name>
              <description>FIR0 Result Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by SAR FIR0 whenever it generates a new result.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.FIR_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the FIR to generate the interrupt source.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_FIR1_RESULT_INT</name>
              <description>FIR1 Result Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by SAR FIR1 whenever it generates a new result.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.FIR_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the FIR to generate the interrupt source.</description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[30:27]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>AC_INT</name>
              <description>Autonomus Controller Interrupt Pending

This interrupt is set by the Autonomus Controller whenever executing a state with  ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CFG1.INTR_SET=1.</description>
              <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
              <access>read-only</access>
            </field>
          </fields>
        </register>
        <register>
