              <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>CTBL1_COMP1</name>
              <description>CTBL1 Comparator 1 Interrupt Request

CTBL1 COMP1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt  if the condition is met (i.e. CTBL_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP1_OUT field). Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_COMP0</name>
              <description>PTC Comparator 0 Interrupt Request

PTC COMP0 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt  if the condition is met (i.e. PTC_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP0_OUT field). Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_COMP1</name>
              <description>PTC Comparator 1 Interrupt Request

PTC COMP1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt  if the condition is met (i.e. PTC_STA_CFG.CFG.COMP1_OUT field). Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_RANGE0</name>
              <description>PTC Post Processor 0 Range Interrupt Request

PTC RANGE0 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt  if the PTC Post Processor RANGE0 condition is met (i.e. PTC_PP_STRUCT0.*). Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>PTC_RANGE1</name>
              <description>PTC Post Processor 1 Range Interrupt Request

PTC RANGE1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt  if the PTC Post Processor RANGE1 condition is met (i.e. PTC_PP_STRUCT1.*). Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE0</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 0 Interrupt Request

DAC0 RANGE0 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC0 RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_0).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE1</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 1 Interrupt Request

DAC0 RANGE1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC0 RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_1).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_RANGE2</name>
              <description>DAC0 Range 2 Interrupt Request

DAC0 RANGE2 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC0 RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. DAC0_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_2).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC0 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[10:10]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_EPOCH</name>
              <description>DAC0 End of Waveform Interrupt Request

DAC0 EPOCH Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when DAC0 finishes a waveform.  This condition is only used if the DAC0 channel (pointed to by the AC when triggered) is configured for LUT mode.  Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC0_EMPTY</name>
              <description>DAC0 Empty Interrupt Request

DAC0 Empty Interrupt:  hardware sets this interrupt when DAC0 empties the contents of its FW_DAC_VAL register.  This interrupt is only generated if DAC0 is configured for FW operation (ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CFG3.DAC0_CHANNEL=FW_CHANNEL.  Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE0</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 0 Interrupt Request

DAC1 RANGE0 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC1 RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_0).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE1</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 1 Interrupt Request

DAC1 RANGE1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC0 RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_1).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_RANGE2</name>
              <description>DAC1 Range 2 Interrupt Request

DAC1 RANGE2 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt if the DAC1 RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. DAC1_STA_CFG.CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_2).  The interrupt is disabled when DAC1 is configured for FW mode.  Write with '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_EPOCH</name>
              <description>DAC1 End of Waveform Interrupt Request

DAC1 EPOCH Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when DAC1 finishes a waveform.  This condition is only used if the DAC1 channel (pointed to by the AC when triggered) is configured for LUT mode.  Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>DAC1_EMPTY</name>
              <description>DAC1 Empty Interrupt Request

DAC1 Empty Interrupt:  hardware sets this interrupt when DAC1 empties the contents of its FW_DAC_VAL register.  This interrupt is only generated if DAC1 is configured for FW operation (ACTRLR.TIMER_TABLE_STRUCT.TT_CFG3.DAC1_CHANNEL=FW_CHANNEL.  Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_DONE</name>
              <description>SAR Sequencer Done Interrupt Request

SAR Done Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR finishes a state in the SAR sequencer table (HS or LP) where SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to STOP.  The interrupt is not generated if the SAR is configured for CONTINUOUS operation (i.e. SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to GO_TO_ENTRY_ADDRESS).  

This interrupt does NOT indicate that the SAR data has been written to the FIFO (the FIFO level interrupts should be used instead). 

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[18:18]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_EOS</name>
              <description>SAR Sequencer EOS Interrupt Request

SAR Sequencer EOS Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR finishes the state in the SAR sequencer table (HS or LP) where SAR.SEQ_HS(LP)_DATA.NEXT_STATE is set to GO_TO_ENTRY_ADDRESS or STOP.  

This interrupt does NOT indicate that the SAR data has been written to the FIFO (the FIFO level interrupts should be used instead). 

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RESULT</name>
              <description>SAR Result Interrupt Request

SAR Result Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR channel writes data into its associated result register.  Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.CHAN_RESULT_UPDATED to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.CHAN_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the SAR to generate the interrupt source.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE0</name>
              <description>SAR Range 0 detection Interrupt Request

SAR Range 0 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR RANGE0 condition is met for a given channel with range 0 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_0).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE1</name>
              <description>SAR Range 1 detection Interrupt Request

SAR Range 1 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR RANGE1 condition is met for a given channel with range 1 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_1).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[22:22]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE2</name>
              <description>SAR Range2 detection Interrupt Request

SAR Range 2 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR RANGE2 condition is met for a given channel with range 2 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_2).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[23:23]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_RANGE3</name>
              <description>SAR Range 3 detection Interrupt Request

SAR Range 3 Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when the SAR RANGE3 condition is met for a given channel with range 3 status enabled (i.e. SAR_STA_CFG.GPIO(MUX)_CHAN_CFG.STATUS_SEL=STATUS_STRUCT_3).   Firmware must read SAR_STA_CFG.RANGE_STATUS to determine which channel generated the interrupt.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_FIR0_RESULT</name>
              <description>FIR0 Result Interrupt Request

SAR FIR0 Result Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when SAR FIR0 generates a new result.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.FIR_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the FIR to generate the interrupt source.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[25:25]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>SAR_FIR1_RESULT</name>
              <description>FIR1 Result Interrupt Request

SAR FIR1 Result Interrupt: hardware sets this interrupt when SAR FIR1 generates a new result.

The corresponding bit in SAR_STA_CFG.FIR_RESULT_MASK must be set in order for the FIR to generate the interrupt source.

Write a '1' to clear bit.</description>
              <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
            <field>
              <name>RSVD</name>
              <description>N/A</description>
              <bitRange>[30:27]</bitRange>
              <access>read-write</access>
            </field>
