                  <description>The cache generates transactions (linefill and eviction Write-Back) that are marked with the given value on hmaster_m.</description>
                  <bitRange>[7:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DIS_CACHE_EN_MAINT</name>
              <description>This signal turns on/off cache enable maintenance.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>DIS_CACHE_EN_MAINT</name>
                  <description>The dis_cache_en_maint port determines if the AHB Cache starts the predefined cache maintenance before the cache enable command would take effect.

dis_cache_en_maint = 1
The AHB Cache does not start any maintenance before applying the cache enable command.

Caution
If cache enable maintenance is disabled, the software must manage the AHB Cache. It must invalidate the content before cache enable if:
- The cache RAMs have not been invalidated after the last power cycle.
- The content is not valid anymore.
Otherwise the data in the cacheable regions is corrupted.

dis_cache_en_maint = 0, 
The AHB Cache starts invalidate all maintenance before it applies the cache enable command that it has received on the APB interface. While the cache enable maintenance is running, the AHB Cache works as if it were disabled. The traffic is not affected. When the invalidation is completed, the next NSEQ transaction on the AHB slave interface is looked up in the cache according to its transaction attributes.

The status of ongoing enable or disable related maintenance can be read at
MAINT_STATUS.ONGOING_EN_DIS status register bit.

The effective enabled or disabled status can be read at MAINT_STATUS.CACHE_ENABLED status register bit.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DIS_CACHE_DIS_MAINT</name>
              <description>This signal turns on/off cache disable maintenance.</description>
              <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>DIS_CACHE_DIS_MAINT</name>
                  <description>The dis_cache_dis_maint determines if the AHB Cache starts the predefined cache maintenance before the cache disable command would take effect.

dis_cache_dis_maint = 1
The AHB Cache drains the internal buffers but does not complete any other maintenance before applying the cache disable command. It only waits for any ongoing Burst on the AHB slave interface to finish.
Caution
If cache disable maintenance is disabled, the software must manage the AHB Cache. If there is any dirty data in the cache RAMs, the software must clean the cache before disable. Otherwise, the main memory is not updated.

dis_cache_dis_maint = 0,
The AHB Cache starts clean all maintenance before it applies the cache disable command that it has received on the APB interface. While this maintenance is running, the AHB Cache stalls the AHB slave interface. After accepting the disable command, the AHB Cache stalls the AHB traffic at the next NSEQ transaction on the AHB slave interface. Then it runs a clean all maintenance process and when it is completed the disable becomes effective and the AHB slave interface is released.

The status of ongoing disable related maintenance can be read at MAINT_STATUS.ONGOING_EN_DIS status register bit.

The effective enabled or disabled status can be read at MAINT_STATUS.CACHE_ENABLED status register bit.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>APB_VIOLATION_RESP</name>
              <description>This signal controls if the APB interface of cache responds with an error to illegal operations on the APB interface. Illegal operations on the APB interface include 
Writing with no full write strobe, Accessing non-word aligned addresses, Instruction accesses, Non-privileged accesses
1 - The Cache responds with an APB error. Read data is masked with zeros and write data is ignored.
0 - The Cache sends an OK response. Read data is masked with zeros and write data is ignored.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>APB_VIOLATION_RESP</name>
                  <description>This bit controls if the APB interface of cache responds with an error to illegal operations on the APB interface. Illegal operations on the APB interface include 
Writing with no full write strobe, Accessing non-word aligned addresses, Instruction accesses, Non-privileged accesses

1 - The AHB Cache responds with an APB error. Read data is masked with zeros and write data is ignored.

0 - The AHB Cache sends an OK response. Read data is masked with zeros and write data is ignored.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>MMIO_ACTIVE_EN</name>
              <description>SW control to put SMIF.6 in active or clock gated mode</description>
              <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>MMIO_ACTIVE_EN</name>
                  <description>SW control to put SMIF.6 in active or clock gated mode

0 - Software writes this value to put SMIF.6 in clock gated mode w/ clocks gated

1 - Software writes this value to put SMIF.6 in active mode w/ clocks from MXSPERI restored</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>POWER_ON_ENABLE</name>
              <description>This signal configures the cache to enable the cache 
automatically after powerup.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>POWER_ON_ENABLE</name>
                  <description>The power_on_enable port can be set so that the AHB Cache is enabled when the LPI interfaces first reach QRUN state after reset. The cache enable bit is combinatorially driven from this bit. With a default of 0, cache comes up disabled upon cold start. 

Before enabling the cache, the SW should set this bit to 1 (if it intends to have the cache come-up enabled after power-down/power-up sequence). The expected behaviour here is that this bit is retained during cache power down and when cache is subsequently powered down and back up, this bit would drive the cache enable bit to 1, thereby automatically enabling the cache. 

If the SW wants to use the cache contents upon return from deep sleep, it should make sure to have set dis_cache_en_maint set to 1 prior to going into deep sleep. Setting dis_cache_en_maint to 1 has an effect of preventing a cache from doing a force invalidate of cache lines prior to enable. 

power_on_enable = 1
The cache is enabled automatically after powerup. This signal is affected by the dis_cache_en_maint.

power_on_enable = 0
The cache waits for software to enable the cache through the APB programming interface.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>MMIO_CACHE_RET_EN</name>
              <description>Controls if cache SRAMs are retained during DPSLP</description>
              <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x1</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>MMIO_CACHE_RET_EN</name>
                  <description>mmio_cache_ret_en = 1
The cache SRAM array retains contents even when cache controller is powered down.

mmio_cache_ret_en = 0
The cache SRAM array does not retain its contents even when cache controller is powered down.

NOTE This bit controls the retention only in DPSLP mode. In DPSLP_RAM and DPSLP_OFF modes cache is never retained regarless of the setting of this bit.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>DIS_PWR_DOWN_MAINT</name>
              <description>This signal turns on/off powerdown maintenance.</description>
              <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>DIS_PWR_DOWN_MAINT</name>
                  <description>The dis_pwr_down_maint port determines if the AHB Cache starts the predefined cache maintenance process before accepting a powerdown request.

dis_pwr_down_maint = 1
If there is no deny condition, the AHB Cache merges any data from its internal buffers into the cache RAMs and accepts the power quiescence request immediately.

dis_pwr_down_maint = 0
The AHB Cache checks the cache clean status whenever a power quiescence request is received through the power Q-Channel interface. If the cache status is clean and otherwise idle, then it accepts the quiescence request. If the cache status is dirty but otherwise idle, it starts a clean all maintenance process. The AHB Cache delays the response and only denies the request if a dirty line is actually found in the cache memory during the maintenance operation. During powerdown maintenance, any AHB activity that would cause a power quiescence request to be denied (except for the maintenance driven activity) results in the maintenance being aborted. Any subsequent quiescence request is denied while the maintenance is in progress. The status of the powerdown maintenance can be monitored
- On the pwr_maintenance output port. This port is driven high only while this process is running.
- By reading the MAINT_STATUS.ONGOING_PWR_MAINT status register bit.</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>REGION0_BASE</name>
              <description>Region 0 Attributes - register 0; 
Region attributes shall be changed exclusively while the SMIF is disabled, and the XIP interfaces are idle</description>
              <addressOffset>0x24</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x7FFF000</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>ADDR</name>
                  <description>Determines the base address of a region 0 within a SMIF external memory address space

The region 0 is hit when REGION0_LIMIT.ENABLE bit is set to 1 and transfer address is inside the range defined by REGION0_BASE.ADDR and REGION0_LIMIT.ADDR:
        REGION0_BASE.ADDR &lt;= address[26:12] &lt;= REGION0_LIMIT.ADDR

Regions are not allowed to overlap; it is SW responsibility to set BASE and LIMIT address values for enabled regions in a way that there is no overlap. HW is not having any protection and will not generate any notification/interrupt in case of wrong configuration.</description>
                  <bitRange>[26:12]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
            <register>
              <name>REGION0_LIMIT</name>
              <description>Region 0 Attributes - register 1; 
Region attributes shall be changed exclusively while the SMIF is disabled, and the XIP interfaces are idle</description>
              <addressOffset>0x28</addressOffset>
              <size>32</size>
              <access>read-write</access>
              <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
              <resetMask>0x7FFF03F</resetMask>
              <fields>
                <field>
                  <name>ENABLE</name>
                  <description>Region 0 Enable

By default, all regions are disabled (all transfers are interpreted as non-cacheable)</description>
                  <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>ATTR</name>
                  <description>This field defines transaction attributes for the region 0 and indicates how the transaction should be handled within a Cache Block:
    [5] R:  Read Allocate
    [4] W: Write Allocate
    [3] L:   Lookup 
    [2] M:  Modifiable
    [1] B:   Bufferable

The following ATTR values are supported:
      5'b00000 - Non-Cacheable, Non-Bufferable (reset value)                 - nCnB
      5'b00001 - Non-Cacheable,        Bufferable                                    - nCB
      5'b01110 - Cacheable,         Write Through,            Write Allocate  - WTWA
      5'b10110 - Cacheable,         Write Through, Read             Allocate  - WTRA
      5'b11110 - Cacheable,         Write Through, Read &amp; Write Allocate  - WTRWA
      5'b01111 - Cacheable,         Write Back   ,              Write Allocate  - WBWA
      5'b10111 - Cacheable,         Write Back   ,  Read             Allocate  - WBRA
      5'b11111 - Cacheable,         Write Back   ,  Read &amp; Write Allocate  - WBRWA

If region is enabled and transaction address is hitting the predefined region address range, corresponding ATTR field is used to overwrite transaction HPROT bits</description>
                  <bitRange>[5:1]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
                <field>
                  <name>ADDR</name>
                  <description>Determines the end address of a region 0 within a SMIF external memory address space

Regions are not allowed to overlap; it is SW responsibility to set BASE and LIMIT address values for enabled regions in a way that there is no overlap. HW is not having any protection and will not generate any notification/interrupt in case of wrong configuration</description>
                  <bitRange>[26:12]</bitRange>
                  <access>read-write</access>
                </field>
              </fields>
            </register>
