              </fields>
            </register>
          </cluster>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <dim>5</dim>
          <dimIncrement>4096</dimIncrement>
          <name>PORT_STRUCT[%s]</name>
          <description>Port structure</description>
          <addressOffset>0x00010000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>PATCH_CTL</name>
            <description>Patch control</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ENABLED</name>
                <description>Patching enable:
'0': Disabled.
'1': Enabled.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PATCH_STATUS</name>
            <description>Patch status</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x80000000</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PATCH_IDX</name>
                <description>Match index of the patch structure that produced the most recent valid address match. The index is in the range [0, PATCH_NR-1].</description>
                <bitRange>[8:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PATCH_VALID</name>
                <description>Validity of PATCH_IDX:
'0': Not valid.
'1': Valid.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PATCH_SROM</name>
            <description>Patch SROM boundary</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>BOUNDARY</name>
                <description>Specifies the boundary between non-patchable and patchable SROM memory locations (the address specifies the first patchable SROM memory location). The SROM is divided into two partitions:
- The first/lower partition is non-patchable. This partition may be used to provide an immutable 'root of trust'.
- The second/high partition is patchable. This partition may be used for FW that requires updates.

Note: Higher bit indices reflect the SROM location in the memory map. When BOUNDARY equals the SROM base address, the complete SROM is patchable. When ADDR equal the SROM base address plus the SROM size, the complete SROM is non-patchable.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PATCH_SRAM</name>
            <description>Patch SRAM address</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>ADDR</name>
                <description>Patch address. This address is the start/base address of a SRAM memory region that holds the patch data. The address should be aligned. Specifically, the address is a multiple of the patch data memory region size. The patch data memory size is defined as follows: PATCH_NR * PATCH_SIZE Bytes (assuming PATCH_NR is a power of two)</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PATCH_CFG</name>
            <description>Patch configuration</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x303FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PATCH_NR</name>
                <description>Patch number; i.e. number of patch structures. Legal range: [0, 512].</description>
                <bitRange>[9:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PATCH_SIZE</name>
                <description>Patch size; i.e. number of Bytes that are 'patched' by a single patch structure:
'0': 8 Bytes.
'1': 16 Bytes.
'2': 32 Bytes.
'3': 64 Bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <dim>512</dim>
            <dimIncrement>4</dimIncrement>
            <name>PATCH_STRUCT_CTL[%s]</name>
            <description>Patch structure control</description>
            <addressOffset>0x800</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>VALID</name>
                <description>Patch structure validity:
'0': Not valid.
'1': Valid.

Note: VALID uses the bit field '0' location, rather than the more typical bit field '31' location. This architecture decision was made to allow for SROM location anywhere in a 32-bit address space, and not require extensive address manipulation when accessing PATCH_CTL.MATCH_ADDR.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MATCH_ADDR</name>
                <description>Match address. SROM accesses are matched/compared to this address. An address match results into patching/redirection of the SROM access (if PATCH_CTL.ENABLED is '1'): the SROM access is redirected to an SRAM access. The PATCH_SRAM register and the 'matching' patch structure index determine the SRAM access address. Specifically, given an index i, the SRAM access address is given by: PATCH_SRAM.ADDR + i * 'patch size', where patch size is the number of Bytes that are patched by a single patch structure (2 ^ (3+CFG.PATCH_SIZE)).

The MATCH_ADDR[] bit range is dependent on two design time configuration parameters:
- The lowest index is determined by PATCH_SIZE. E.g., a 8 Byte patch size uses '3' as the lowest bit index, a 16 Byte patch size uses '4' as the lowest bit index, etc. Unused lower bit indices are '0'.
- The highest index is determined by the SROM size as specified by SROM_MASK (the next power of two multiple of the SROM size). E.g., a 0xffff:0000 mask specifies a 64 KByte SROM size and uses '15' as the highest bit index, a 0xfff0:0000 mask specifies a 1 MByte SROM size and uses '19' as the highest bit index, etc. Unused higher bit indices reflect the SROM location in the address space.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>PPU_SOCMEM</name>
          <description>Power Policy Unit Registers for SOCMEM PPU</description>
          <addressOffset>0x00020000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>PWPR</name>
            <description>Power Policy Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x108</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x10F110F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PWR_POLICY</name>
                <description>Power mode policy.  When static power mode transitions are enabled, PWR_DYN_EN is set to 0, this is the target power mode for the PPU.  When dynamic power mode transitions are enabled, PWR_DYN_EN is set to 1, this is the minimum power mode for the PPU.

This PPU supports the following modes: OFF(0), MEM_RET(2), ON(8).  Do not use WARM_RST(9) or other unsupported modes.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PWR_DYN_EN</name>
                <description>Power mode dynamic transition enable.  When this bit is set to 1 dynamic transitions are enabled for power modes, allowing transitions to be initiated by changes on power mode DEVACTIVE inputs.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>LOCK_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OP_POLICY</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OP_DYN_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PMER</name>
            <description>Power Mode Emulation Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x1</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>EMU_EN</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PWSR</name>
            <description>Power Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x10F110F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PWR_STATUS</name>
                <description>Power mode status.  These bits reflect the current power mode of the PPU.  See PPU_PWPR.PWR_POLICY for power mode enumeration.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PWR_DYN_STATUS</name>
                <description>Power mode dynamic transition status.  When set to 1 power mode dynamic transitions are enabled.  There might be a delay in dynamic transitions becoming active or inactive if the PPU is transitioning when PWR_DYN_EN is programmed.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>LOCK_STATUS</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OP_STATUS</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OP_DYN_STATUS</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[24:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DISR</name>
            <description>Device Interface Input Current Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFF0007FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PWR_DEVACTIVE_STATUS</name>
                <description>Status of the power mode DEVACTIVE inputs.

There is one bit for each device interface Q-Channel DEVQACTIVE.  For example, bit 0 is for Q-channel device 0 DEVQACTIVE.  Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OP_DEVACTIVE_STATUS</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>MISR</name>
            <description>Miscellaneous Input Current Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFF01</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PCSMPACCEPT_STATUS</name>
                <description>The status of the PCSMPACCEPT input.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEVACCEPT_STATUS</name>
                <description>Status of the device interface DEVACCEPT inputs.  

There is one bit for each device interface DEVQACCEPTn.  For example, bit 8 is for Q-Channel 0 DEVQACCEPTn and bit 9 for Q-Channel 1 DEVQACCEPTn.   Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEVDENY_STATUS</name>
                <description>Status of the device interface DEVDENY inputs.  

There is one bit for each device interface DEVQDENY.  For example, bit 16 is for Q-Channel 0 DEVQDENY, and bit 17 for Q-Channel 1 DEVQDENY.  Refer to PPU_IDR0.DEVCHAN for device channel enumeration.</description>
                <bitRange>[23:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>STSR</name>
            <description>Stored Status Register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
