          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DIEPTSIZ8</name>
            <description>This register reflects the Transfer Size of the IN Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.

Note: This register exists for an endpoint i if the OTG_EP_DIR_i parameter is 0 or 1 for that endpoint.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet from the
external memory is written to the TxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)

Indicates the total number of USB packets that constitute the Transfer Size amount of data for endpoint 0.

This field is decremented every time a packet (maximum size or short packet) is read from the TxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>MC</name>
                <description>MC

Applies to IN endpoints only.

For periodic IN endpoints, this field indicates the number of packets that must be transmitted per microframe on the USB. The core uses this field to calculate the data PID for isochronous IN endpoints. 
 - 2'b01: 1 packet 
 - 2'b10: 2 packets 
 - 2'b11: 3 packets 
For non-periodic IN endpoints, this field is valid only in Internal DMA mode. It specifies the number of packets the core must fetchfor an IN endpoint before it switches to the endpoint pointed to by the Next Endpoint field of the Device Endpoint-n Control register (DIEPCTLn.NextEp)</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PACKETONE</name>
                    <description>1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PACKETTHREE</name>
                    <description>3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>PACKETTWO</name>
                    <description>2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DIEPDMA8</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the IN Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.

Note: This register exists for an endpoint i if the OTG_EP_DIR_i parameter is 0 or 1 for that endpoint.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DTXFSTS8</name>
            <description>This register reflects the status of the IN Endpoint Transmit FIFO Status Register 8 of the Device controller.

Note: This register exists for an endpoint i if the OTG_EP_DIR_i parameter is 0 or 1 for that endpoint.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA18</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x100</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>INEPTXFSPCAVAIL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[15:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DIEPDMAB8</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address of the IN Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.

Note: This register exists for an endpoint i if the OTG_EP_DIR_i parameter is 0 or 1 for that endpoint.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xA1C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL0</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of the OUT Endpoint 0 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB00</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x8000</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xCC3E8003</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The maximum packet size for control OUT endpoint 0 is the same as what is programmed in control IN Endpoint 0.
 - 2'b00: 64 bytes
 - 2'b01: 32 bytes
 - 2'b10: 16 bytes
 - 2'b11: 8 bytes</description>
                <bitRange>[1:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BYTE16</name>
                    <description>16 bytes</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BYTE32</name>
                    <description>32 bytes</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BYTE64</name>
                    <description>64 bytes</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BYTE8</name>
                    <description>8 bytes</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

This bit is always set to 1, indicating that a control endpoint 0 is always active in all configurations and interfaces.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint 0</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit, the core
stops receiving data, even If there is space in the RxFIFO to
accommodate the incoming packet. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with
an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

Hardcoded to 2'b00 for control.</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Control 0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL0</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL0</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when
a SETUP token is received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit or
Global OUT NAK is Set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes
priority. Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always
responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Stall Handshake</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Stall</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)

A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLEAR</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOCLEAR</name>
                    <description>No action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.
Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK handshakes on an endpoint.
The core can also set bit on a Transfer Completed interrupt, or after a SETUP is received on the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOSET</name>
                    <description>No action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>SET</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

The application cannot disable control OUT endpoint 0.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint disable</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, for OUT endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled (such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode)this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
 - The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 -- SETUP Phase Done
 -- Endpoint Disabled
 -- Transfer Completed
Note: In DMA mode, this bit must be set for the core to transfer SETUP data packets into memory and it will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT0</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 0 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB08</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 - For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 - For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
Note: In DMA mode, this bit must be set for the core to transfer SETUP data packets into memory. When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint
was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. 

This interrupt is asserted when the core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.
The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.
In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ0</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 0 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x6008007F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)

This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[19:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SUPCNT</name>
                <description>SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ONEPACKET</name>
                    <description>1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>THREEPACKET</name>
                    <description>3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>TWOPACKET</name>
                    <description>2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA0</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 0 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB0</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 0 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB1C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL1</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 1 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB20</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL1</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL1</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT1</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 1 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB28</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ1</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 1 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB30</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA1</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 1 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB34</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB1</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 1 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB3C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL2</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 2 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB40</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL2</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL2</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT2</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 2 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB48</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ2</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 2 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB50</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA2</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 2 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB54</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB2</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 2 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB5C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL3</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 3 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB60</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL3</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL3</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT3</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 3 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB68</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ3</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 3 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB70</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA3</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 3 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB74</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB3</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 3 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB7C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL4</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 4 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB80</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL4</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL4</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT4</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 4 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB88</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ4</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 4 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB90</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA4</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 4 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB94</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB4</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 4 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xB9C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL5</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 5 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBA0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL5</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL5</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT5</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 5 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBA8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ5</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 5 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBB0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA5</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 5 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBB4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB5</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 5 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBBC</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL6</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 6 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBC0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL6</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL6</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT6</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 6 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBC8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ6</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 6 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBD0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA6</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 6 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBD4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB6</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 6 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBDC</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL7</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 7 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBE0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL7</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL7</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT7</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 7 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBE8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ7</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 7 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBF0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA7</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 7 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBF4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB7</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 7 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xBFC</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPCTL8</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the characteristics of OUT Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC00</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFC3F87FF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>MPS</name>
                <description>Maximum Packet Size (MPS)

The application must program this field with the maximum packet size for the current
logical endpoint. This value is in bytes.</description>
                <bitRange>[10:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USBACTEP</name>
                <description>USB Active Endpoint (USBActEP)

Indicates whether this endpoint is active in the current configuration and interface. The
core clears this bit for all endpoints (other than EP 0) after detecting a USB reset. After
receiving the SetConfiguration and SetInterface commands, the application must
program endpoint registers accordingly and set this bit.</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Not Active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>USB Active Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DPID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID active</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Data PID not active</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKSTS</name>
                <description>NAK Status (NAKSts)

Indicates the following:
 - 1'b0: The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status.
 - 1'b1: The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint.
When either the application or the core sets this bit:
 - The core stops receiving any data on an OUT endpoint, even if there is space in the RxFIFO to accommodate the incoming packet.
 - For non-isochronous IN endpoints: The core stops transmitting any data on an IN endpoint, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
 - For isochronous IN endpoints: The core sends out a zero-length data packet, even if there data is available in the TxFIFO.
Irrespective of this bit's setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting NAK handshakes on this endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NONNAK</name>
                    <description>The core is transmitting non-NAK handshakes based on the FIFO status</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPTYPE</name>
                <description>Endpoint Type (EPType)

This is the transfer type supported by this logical endpoint.
 - 2'b00: Control
 - 2'b01: Isochronous
 - 2'b10: Bulk
 - 2'b11: Interrupt</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>BULK</name>
                    <description>Bulk</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CONTROL</name>
                    <description>Control</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INTERRUPT</name>
                    <description>Interrupt</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ISOCHRONOUS</name>
                    <description>Isochronous</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNP</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD0SnpDOEPCTL8</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RSVD1SnpDOEPCTL8</name>
                    <description>N/A</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STALL</name>
                <description>STALL Handshake (Stall)

Applies to non-control, non-isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.

The application sets this bit to stall all tokens from the USB host to this endpoint. If a
NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT NAK is set along with this bit, the
STALL bit takes priority. Only the application can clear this bit, never the core.

Applies to control endpoints only.

The application can only set this bit, and the core clears it, when a SETUP token is
received for this endpoint. If a NAK bit, Global Non-periodic IN NAK, or Global OUT
NAK is set along with this bit, the STALL bit takes priority. Irrespective of this bit's
setting, the core always responds to SETUP data packets with an ACK handshake.</description>
                <bitRange>[21:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All Active Tokens</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>STALL All non-active tokens</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CNAK</name>
                <description>Clear NAK (CNAK)
A write to this bit clears the NAK bit for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[26:26]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Clear NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SNAK</name>
                <description>Set NAK (SNAK)

A write to this bit sets the NAK bit for the endpoint.

Using this bit, the application can control the transmission of NAK
handshakes on an endpoint. The core can also set this bit for an
endpoint after a SETUP packet is received on that endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[27:27]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Set NAK</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Set NAK</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD0PID</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA0 PID or Do not force Even Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA0 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETD1PID</name>
                <description>Set DATA1 PID (SetD1PID)
 - Applies to interrupt and bulk IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the Endpoint Data PID (DPID) field in this register to DATA1.
 - This field is applicable both for scatter-gather DMA mode and non scatter-gather DMA mode.
Reset: 1'b0

Set Odd (micro)frame (SetOddFr)
 - Applies to isochronous IN and OUT endpoints only.
 - Writing to this field sets the even and odd (micro)frame (EO_FrNum) field to odd (micro)frame.
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>write-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disables Set DATA1 PID or Do not force Odd Frame</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Set Endpoint Data PID to DATA1 or Sets EO_FrNum field to odd &lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID==1&gt;frame&lt;/ct&gt;&lt;ct:DWC_DESIGN_ID!=1&gt;(micro)Frame&lt;/ct&gt;</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDIS</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disable (EPDis)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

The application sets this bit to stop transmitting/receiving data on an endpoint, even
before the transfer for that endpoint is complete. The application must wait for the
Endpoint Disabled interrupt before treating the endpoint as disabled. The core clears
this bit before setting the Endpoint Disabled interrupt. The application must set this bit
only if Endpoint Enable is already set for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[30:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Disable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPENA</name>
                <description>Endpoint Enable (EPEna)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled,
 - For IN endpoints this bit indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer with data ready to transmit is setup.
 - For OUT endpoint it indicates that the descriptor structure and data buffer to receive data is setup.
When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled such as for buffer-pointer based DMA mode:
 - For IN endpoints, this bit indicates that data is ready to be transmitted on the endpoint.
 - For OUT endpoints, this bit indicates that the application has allocated the memory to start receiving data from the USB.
The core clears this bit before setting any of the following interrupts on this endpoint:
 - SETUP Phase Done
 - Endpoint Disabled
 - Transfer Completed 
Note: For control endpoints in DMA mode, this bit must be set for the controller to transfer SETUP data packets to the memory. This bit will not be cleared on Transfer Completed interrupt of the SETUP packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:31]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Enable Endpoint</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Action</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPINT8</name>
            <description>This register contains the interrupts for the OUT Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC08</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFB7F</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERCOMPL</name>
                <description>Transfer Completed Interrupt (XferCompl)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled
 -- For IN endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the descriptor is moved from external system memory to internal FIFO.
 -- For OUT endpoint this field indicates that the requested data from the internal FIFO is moved to external system memory. This interrupt is generated only when the corresponding endpoint descriptor is closed, and the IOC bit for the corresponding descriptor is Set.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is disabled, this field indicates that the programmed transfer is complete on the AHB as well as on the USB, for this endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Transfer Complete Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EPDISBLD</name>
                <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt (EPDisbld)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This bit indicates that the endpoint is disabled per the application's request.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Endpoint Disabled Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHBERR</name>
                <description>AHB Error (AHBErr)

Applies to IN and OUT endpoints.

This is generated only in Internal DMA mode when there is an AHB error during an AHB read/write. The application can read the corresponding endpoint DMA address register to get the error address. For details, see 'AHB Error Handling' section in the Programming Guide.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>AHB Error interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No AHB Error Interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SETUP</name>
                <description>SETUP Phase Done (SetUp)

Applies to control OUT endpoints only.

Indicates that the SETUP phase for the control endpoint is
complete and no more back-to-back SETUP packets were
received for the current control transfer. On this interrupt, the
application can decode the received SETUP data packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No SETUP Phase Done</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTTKNEPDIS</name>
                <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled (OUTTknEPdis)

Applies only to control OUT endpoints.

Indicates that an OUT token was received when the endpoint was not yet enabled. This interrupt is asserted on the endpoint for which the OUT token was received.</description>
                <bitRange>[4:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Token Received When Endpoint Disabled</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STSPHSERCVD</name>
                <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write (StsPhseRcvd)

This interrupt is valid only for Control OUT endpoints.

This interrupt is generated only after the core has transferred all
the data that the host has sent during the data phase of a control
write transfer, to the system memory buffer.

The interrupt indicates to the application that the host has
switched from data phase to the status phase of a Control Write
transfer. The application can use this interrupt to ACK or STALL
the Status phase, after it has decoded the data phase. This is
applicable only in Case of Scatter Gather DMA mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Status Phase Received for Control Write</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BACK2BACKSETUP</name>
                <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received (Back2BackSETup)

Applies to Control OUT endpoints only.

This bit indicates that the core has received more than three
back-to-back SETUP packets for this particular endpoint. For
information about handling this interrupt,</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No Back-to-Back SETUP Packets Received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OUTPKTERR</name>
                <description>OUT Packet Error (OutPktErr)

Applies to OUT endpoints Only

This interrupt is valid only when thresholding is enabled. This interrupt is asserted when the
core detects an overflow or a CRC error for non-Isochronous OUT packet.</description>
                <bitRange>[8:8]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No OUT Packet Error</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BNAINTR</name>
                <description>BNA (Buffer Not Available) Interrupt (BNAIntr)

This bit is valid only when Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled.

The core generates this interrupt when the descriptor accessed
is not ready for the Core to process, such as Host busy or DMA
done</description>
                <bitRange>[9:9]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BNA interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTDRPSTS</name>
                <description>Packet Drop Status (PktDrpSts)

This bit indicates to the application that an ISOC OUT packet has been dropped. This
bit does not have an associated mask bit and does not generate an interrupt.

Dependency: This bit is valid in non Scatter/Gather DMA mode when periodic transfer
interrupt feature is selected.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Packet Drop Status interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>BBLEERR</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (BbleErr)

The core generates this interrupt when babble is received for the endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No BbleErr interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NAKINTRPT</name>
                <description>NAK Interrupt (NAKInterrupt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NAK is transmitted or received by the device.

In case of isochronous IN endpoints the interrupt gets generated when a zero length
packet is transmitted due to un-availability of data in the TXFifo.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NAK Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NAK interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>NYETINTRPT</name>
                <description>NYET Interrupt (NYETIntrpt)

The core generates this interrupt when a NYET response is transmitted for a non isochronous OUT endpoint.</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>NYET Interrupt</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>No NYET interrupt</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>STUPPKTRCVD</name>
                <description>Setup Packet Received

Applicable for Control OUT Endpoints in only in the Buffer DMA Mode

Set by the controller, this bit indicates that this buffer holds 8 bytes of
setup data. There is only one Setup packet per buffer. On receiving a
Setup packet, the controller closes the buffer and disables the
corresponding endpoint. The application has to re-enable the endpoint to
receive any OUT data for the Control Transfer and reprogram the buffer
start address.

Note: Because of the above behavior, the controller can receive any
number of back to back setup packets and one buffer for every setup
packet is used.
 - 1'b0: No Setup packet received
 - 1'b1: Setup packet received
Reset: 1'b0</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>NOT_RCVD</name>
                    <description>No Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>RCVD</name>
                    <description>Setup packet received</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPTSIZ8</name>
            <description>This register contains the Transfer Size for the OUT Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x7FFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>XFERSIZE</name>
                <description>Transfer Size (XferSize)

Indicates the transfer size in bytes for endpoint 0. The core
interrupts the application only after it has exhausted the transfer
size amount of data. The transfer size can be Set to the
maximum packet size of the endpoint, to be interrupted at the
end of each packet.

The core decrements this field every time a packet is read from
the RxFIFO and written to the external memory.</description>
                <bitRange>[18:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PKTCNT</name>
                <description>Packet Count (PktCnt)
This field is decremented to zero after a packet is written into the RxFIFO.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:19]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RXDPID</name>
                <description>RxDPID

Applies to isochronous OUT endpoints only.

This is the data PID received in the last packet for this endpoint.
 - 2'b00: DATA0
 - 2'b01: DATA2
 - 2'b10: DATA1
 - 2'b11: MDATA
SETUP Packet Count (SUPCnt)

Applies to control OUT Endpoints only.

This field specifies the number of back-to-back SETUP data
packets the endpoint can receive.
 - 2'b01: 1 packet
 - 2'b10: 2 packets
 - 2'b11: 3 packets</description>
                <bitRange>[30:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA0</name>
                    <description>DATA0</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA1PACKET2</name>
                    <description>DATA1 or 2 packets</description>
                    <value>2</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DATA2PACKET1</name>
                    <description>DATA2 or 1 packet</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>MDATAPACKET3</name>
                    <description>MDATA or 3 packets</description>
                    <value>3</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMA8</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Address for the OUT Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMAADDR</name>
                <description>Holds the start address of the external memory for storing or fetching endpoint
data.

Note: For control endpoints, this field stores control OUT data packets as well as
SETUP transaction data packets. When more than three SETUP packets are
received back-to-back, the SETUP data packet in the memory is overwritten.

This register is incremented on every AHB transaction. The application can give
only a DWORD-aligned address.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is not enabled, the application programs the start address value in this field.
 - When Scatter/Gather DMA mode is enabled, this field indicates the base pointer for the descriptor list.</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>DOEPDMAB8</name>
            <description>This register contains the DMA Buffer Address for the OUT Endpoint 8 of the Device controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC1C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x0</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DMABUFFERADDR</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PCGCCTL</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the Power and Clock Gating characteristics of the controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xE00</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xE9</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>STOPPCLK</name>
                <description>Stop Pclk (StopPclk)
 - The application sets this bit to stop the PHY clock (phy_clk) when the USB is suspended, the session is not valid, or the device is disconnected.
 - The application clears this bit when the USB is resumed or a new session starts.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>Disable Stop Pclk</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>Enable Stop Pclk</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSTPDWNMODULE</name>
                <description>Reset Power-Down Modules (RstPdwnModule)

This bit is valid only in Partial Power-Down mode. 
 - The application sets this bit when the power is turned off. 
 - The application clears this bit after the power is turned on and the PHY clock is up.

Note: The R/W of all core registers are possible only when this bit is set to 1b0.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>OFF</name>
                    <description>Power is turned off</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ON</name>
                    <description>Power is turned on</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>ENBL_L1GATING</name>
                <description>Enable Sleep Clock Gating 

If this bit is set, core internal clock gating is enabled sleep state 
if utmi_l1_suspend_n cannot be asserted by the core.

The PHY clock will not be gated in sleep state if Enbl_L1Gating is not set.</description>
                <bitRange>[5:5]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>The PHY clock is not gated in Sleep state</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>The Core internal clock gating is enabled in Sleep state</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PHYSLEEP</name>
                <description>PHY In Sleep 

Indicates that the PHY is in Sleep State.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Phy in Sleep state</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Phy not in Sleep state</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>L1SUSPENDED</name>
                <description>L1 Deep Sleep 

Indicates that the PHY is in deep sleep when in L1 state.</description>
                <bitRange>[7:7]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Deep Sleep</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>INACTIVE</name>
                    <description>Non Deep Sleep</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PCGCCTL1</name>
            <description>This register is used to control the Power and Clock Gating characteristics of the controller.</description>
            <addressOffset>0xE04</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>GATEEN</name>
                <description>Enable Active Clock Gating (GateEn) 
 The application programs GateEn to enable Active Clock Gating feature for the PHY and AHB clocks.
 - 1b0: Disable Active Clock Gating  (Default)
 - 1b1: Enable Active Clock Gating</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>ACG disable</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>ACG enable</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>COUNTER</name>
                <description>Count to Gate Clock (CntGateClk) 
 CntGateClk indicates to the Controller how many PHY Clock cycles and AHB Clock cycles of 'IDLE' (no activity) the Controller will wait for before Gating the respective PHY and AHB clocks internal to the Controller.

 - 2b00:   64 clocks  (Default).
 - 2b01:  128 clocks</description>
                <bitRange>[2:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLOCKS128</name>
                    <description>CLOCKS128</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>CLOCKS64</name>
                    <description>CLOCKS64</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RAMGATEEN</name>
                <description>RAM Clock Gating Enable (RAMGateEn) 
 The application programs RAMGateEn to enable RAM Clock Gating in the ACG feature.
 - 1b0: Disable RAM Clock Gating  (Default)
 - 1b1: Enable RAM Clock Gating</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
                <enumeratedValues>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>DISABLED</name>
                    <description>RAM Clock Gating disable</description>
                    <value>0</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                  <enumeratedValue>
                    <name>ENABLED</name>
                    <description>RAM Clock Gating enable</description>
                    <value>1</value>
                  </enumeratedValue>
                </enumeratedValues>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
        <cluster>
          <name>SS</name>
          <description>USBHS Subsystem, Controller, PHY control,configuration and status registers</description>
          <addressOffset>0x00040000</addressOffset>
          <register>
            <name>SUBSYSTEM_CTL</name>
            <description>Subsystem level control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x0</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x32</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>SS_ENABLE</name>
                <description>1'b1: Subsystem enable after power on reset to indicate both analog and digital power supply,all top level clocks,resets are stable to configure Controller core mode. 1'b0: Subsystem disable</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USB_MODE</name>
                <description>1'b1:USB Subsystem is set to Host mode.1'b0: Subsystem is set to Device mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USB_PHY_POWER_DOWN</name>
                <description>1'b1:In the PHY during Sleep , the bias,PLL blocks are powered down and in Suspend , REFCLK_LOGIC also powered down. 1'b0:All the three blocks remain powered during Sleep and Suspend modes.</description>
                <bitRange>[2:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>AHB_MASTER_SYNC</name>
                <description>1'b1:Enables AHB Master interface of Controller synchronisation to 'mmio_clk_hf' clock.1'b0: Disables AHB5 Master interface synchronisation such that 100 MHz generated out of 'clk_hf' is used.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:3]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USB_CTRL_SCALEDOWN_MODE</name>
                <description>2'b00: Disables all scale-downs,actual timing value are used for synthesis.2'b01:Enables scale-down of all timing except Device mode.2'b10:Enables scale-down of Device mode suspend and resume timing values only.2'b11:Enables bit 0 and 1 scale-down timing values useful for simulations</description>
                <bitRange>[5:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SS_FORCED_SHUTDOWN</name>
                <description>1'b1: Forces shutdown of Controller resulting in data corruption from/to of Controller to the PHY resulting in USBHS moving to Q-state by accepting Q-channel request. 1'b0: Subsystem in normal operational mode.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD_1</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:7]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PHY_FUNC_CTL_1</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- PHY functional mode control register 1</description>
            <addressOffset>0x4</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x14CF5BE</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PLL_B_TUNE</name>
                <description>PHY PLL reference clock frequency tuning enable to double bandwidth of the PLL 1'b1: Enable ,1'b0: Disable</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PLL_FSEL</name>
                <description>PHY PLL reference clock frequency selection(MHz). If BANDWIDTH_TUNE is 1'b0 ,then 3'b000 is 9.6, 3'b001 is 10, 3'b010 is 12 and 3'b111 is 50 .Else  3'b000 is 19.2, 3'b001 is 20, 3'b010 is 24. Other combination of values are invlaid.</description>
                <bitRange>[3:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DISCONNECT_THRESHOLD</name>
                <description>Voltage level threshold to detect a disconnect event. 3'b000 : -4.33  percent , 3'b001: -2.93  percent , 3'b010: -1.48 percent, 3'b011:0 percent,3'b100: +1.36 percent, 3'b101: + 2.98 percent, 3'b110 : +4.67 percent ,3'b111: +6.45 percent.</description>
                <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>SQUELCH_THRESHOLD</name>
                <description>Voltage level threshold to detect valid High-Speed data. 3'b000 : +16.9  percent , 3'b001: +10.95  percent , 3'b010: +5.86 percent, 3'b011:0 percent,3'b100: -5.03 percent, 3'b101: -11.41 percent, 3'b110 : -16.92 percent ,3'b111: -22.75 percent.</description>
                <bitRange>[9:7]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>CHARGER_TYPE_DETECTION</name>
                <description>Data detect voltage threshold adjustment during charger type detection. 2'b00:+10.2 percent , 2'b01:0  percent, 2'b10:-10.2 percent, 2'b11: -20.75 percent</description>
                <bitRange>[11:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TX_HS_CROSSOVER</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[13:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>FS_LS_SOURCE_IMPEDANCE</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[17:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HS_DC_VOLTAGE</name>
                <description>HS DC voltage( percent) level adjustment.4'b000:-6.06,4'b0001:-4.04,4'b0010:-2.03,4'b0011:0</description>
                <bitRange>[21:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HS_TX_RISETUNE</name>
                <description>HS transmitter rise/fall time( percent).2'b00:+1.06, 2'b01:0, 2'b10:-1.9, 2'b11:-2.33</description>
                <bitRange>[23:22]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>USB_SOURCE_IMPEDANCE</name>
                <description>USB source impedance (Ohm). 2'b00:+3.01, 2'b01:0, 2'b10:2.68, 2'b11:5.01</description>
                <bitRange>[25:24]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HS_TX_PRE_EMPHASIS_CURRENT</name>
                <description>HS transmitter pre-emphasis current control. 2'b00:0 ,2'b01:1x,2'b10:2x,2'b11:3x</description>
                <bitRange>[27:26]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>HS_TX_PRE_EMPHASIS_DURATION</name>
                <description>HS transmitter pre-emphasis duration control. 1'b0:2x long pre-emphasis, 1'b1:1x short pre-emphasis.</description>
                <bitRange>[28:28]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PORT_RESET</name>
                <description>PHY port reset. 1'b0:Transmitter ,Receiver FSMs are operational and line_state becomes sequential after 11 PHY clock cycles. 1'b1:Transmitter ,Receiver FSMs are reset and line_state combinatorily reflects the state of SE-receivers.</description>
                <bitRange>[29:29]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>PLL_I_TUNE</name>
                <description>PHY PLL Integral path tune.2'b00:1.0x ,2'b01:2.0x,2'b10:3.0x,2'b11:4.0x</description>
                <bitRange>[31:30]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PHY_FUNC_CTL_2</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- PHY functional mode control register 2</description>
            <addressOffset>0x8</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x7BC</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>PLL_P_TUNE</name>
                <description>PHY PLL Proportioanl path tune.4'b1100:10.0x(design default)</description>
                <bitRange>[3:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>OTG_TUNE</name>
                <description>VBUS Valid Threshold Adjustment.3'b011: 0, (design default)</description>
                <bitRange>[6:4]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RES_TUNING_CODE</name>
                <description>This bus is the resistor tuning code for the high-speed DP0 and DM0 source impedance of the USB 2.0 femtoPHY.
The input values for this bus could be driven by ATE during
characterization or production, and by an EFUSE block during product
operation.
All values from 4'b1111 to 4'b0000 are valid.4'b1111: provides lowest impedance on DP0 and DM0.4'b0000: provides the highest impedance on DP0 and DM0</description>
                <bitRange>[10:7]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RES_TUNING_SEL</name>
                <description>This signal selects the tuning method for the high-speed DP0 and DM0 source impedance of the USB 2.0 femtoPHY.1: Internal digital calibration code is used for tuning the high-speed source impedance.0: The RCALCODE0[3:0] value is used for tuning the high-speed source impedance.</description>
                <bitRange>[11:11]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>EFUSE_SEL</name>
                <description>This signal selects the tuning method for the high-speed DP0 and DM0 source impedance of the USB 2.0 femtoPHY.1: Internal digital calibration code is used for tuning the high-speed source impedance.0: The RCALCODE0[3:0] value is used for tuning the high-speed source impedance.</description>
                <bitRange>[12:12]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD_2</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:13]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>PHY_TEST_CTL</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- PHY test mode control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0xC</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0xC0000</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_PULSE</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode non-continous clocking signal</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_DATAOUT_SEL0</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode data output selection. 1'b1: Mode-defined test register contents are output. 1'b0:Mode-defined, internally generated signals are ouptut.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_DATAIN0</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode data write bus. Bits 0 to 3 are data, 4 to 7 are corresponding write enables</description>
                <bitRange>[9:2]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_ADDR0</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode register address bus.</description>
                <bitRange>[13:10]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_BURNIN</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode burn-in control. 1'b1: Enable(PHY performs power on reset,enters HS loopback mode to transmit alternate 1's and 0's until disabled. 1'b0:Disable</description>
                <bitRange>[14:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_SCAN_EN</name>
                <description>USB PHY scan test (at-speed and stuck-at fault)mode. 1'b1: Enable.1'b0:Disable</description>
                <bitRange>[15:15]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_IDDQ_EN</name>
                <description>USB PHY IDDQ test control 1'b1: Enable.1'b0:Disable</description>
                <bitRange>[16:16]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_LOOPBACK_EN</name>
                <description>USB PHY loopback test control 1'b1: Enable.1'b0:Disable</description>
                <bitRange>[17:17]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_ANALOGPIN_SEL</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[19:18]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>TEST_ATERESET</name>
                <description>USB PHY test mode ATE reset. 1'b1 : Disables PHYCLOCK0 ,FREECLK outputs. 1'b0:PHYCLOCK0 ,FREECLK outputs are available within(maximum duration) 16.33 ns if UTMI+ interface is 8 bits else 24.66 ns</description>
                <bitRange>[20:20]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD_3</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:21]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>CONTROLLER_DDFT_CTL</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- Controller DDFT control register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x10</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0xFFFFFFFF</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>DEBUG_HCLK_PROBES_COUNT</name>
                <description>USB Controller AHB clock domain internal signal probe counter value for debug[6'd0 to 6'd61]</description>
                <bitRange>[5:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>DEBUG_PCLK_PROBES_COUNT</name>
                <description>USB Controller PHY clock domain internal signal probe counter value for debug[8'd0 to 8'd218]</description>
                <bitRange>[13:6]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>RSVD_4</name>
                <description>N/A</description>
                <bitRange>[31:14]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SS</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- Interrupt register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x14</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_HCLK_GATED</name>
                <description>Subsystem and USB Controller AHB clock are gated after Q-state request is accepted either after end of transfer or forceful shutdown request from APPCPUSS</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_REQ_ERR</name>
                <description>Subsystem and USB Controller AHB clock are gated after Q-state request is accepted either after end of transfer or forceful shutdown request from APPCPUSS</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SS_SET</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- Interrupt set register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x18</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_HCLK_GATED</name>
                <description>Write 1'b1 to set corresponding bit in interrupt register.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_REQ_ERR</name>
                <description>Write 1'b1 to set corresponding bit in interrupt register.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SS_MASK</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- Interrupt mask register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x1C</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-write</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_HCLK_GATED</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt register.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_REQ_ERR</name>
                <description>Mask bit for corresponding bit in interrupt register.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-write</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
          <register>
            <name>INTR_SS_MASKED</name>
            <description>Subsystem level- Interrupt masked register</description>
            <addressOffset>0x20</addressOffset>
            <size>32</size>
            <access>read-only</access>
            <resetValue>0x0</resetValue>
            <resetMask>0x3</resetMask>
            <fields>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_HCLK_GATED</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding interrupt and mask bits.SW need to set CTL.SS_FORCED_SHUTDOWN bit one clk_pwr cycle before PERI asserts Qrequest otherwise subsystem will deny the Qrequest. This interrupt indicates Subsystem accepts Q-state request and its functional clock (mmio_clk_sys) is internally gated and is in DEEPSLEEP power mode. Its PERI/SRSS responsbility to gate clk_pwr,clk_phy_pll_ref and assert rst_pwr_act_n as required. Renabling Subsystem requires first QExit-&gt;QEntry from PERI then Subsystem programming.</description>
                <bitRange>[0:0]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
              <field>
                <name>QCH_REQ_ERR</name>
                <description>Logical and of corresponding interrupt and mask bits.</description>
                <bitRange>[1:1]</bitRange>
                <access>read-only</access>
              </field>
            </fields>
          </register>
        </cluster>
      </registers>
    </peripheral>
  </peripherals>
</device>